<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anaximander</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 610–546 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anaximander of Miletus was a Greek philosopher of the 6th century BCE and a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> in the Milesian school. He held that the origin of all things was the apeiron, 'the boundless', an indefinite substance from which opposites separate. He is credited with drawing one of the first maps of the known world and with an early theory that living things first arose in water.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anaximander.jpg" alt="Anaximander" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Pietro Bellotti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Bellotti_(attr)_Anaximander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
